diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0105-nss_core-back-fw-empty-buffer-payloads-with-kmalloc.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0105-nss_core-back-fw-empty-buffer-payloads-with-kmalloc.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..504ba3de
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0105-nss_core-back-fw-empty-buffer-payloads-with-kmalloc.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: back firmware empty-buffer payloads with kmalloc
+
+The empty-buffer pool skbs are handed to the NSS firmware, which keeps
+them in custody indefinitely (n2h_empty_pool_buf + n2h_wifi_pool_buf,
+~14.5k skbs on a two-radio IPQ8074 with the wifili pool configured).
+dev_alloc_skb() carves their ~2KB payloads out of the shared per-CPU
+page-frag caches, i.e. 32KB compound chunks holding up to 13 buffers,
+interleaved with short-lived traffic skbs. Every payload the firmware
+keeps pins its entire chunk once the neighbouring buffers are freed,
+and the pool slowly diffuses across chunks as buffers cycle through
+the firmware.
+
+Measured on a 512MB AX3600 after 5h of uptime (full-pfn page census):
+6098 order-3 frag chunks = 195MB pinned, holding ~14.4k custody
+payloads = ~34MB of actual buffer memory - a ~5x amplification that
+plateaus only when most custody buffers sit in near-empty chunks. A
+100s saturation burst diluted the pool further (+334 chunks for +135
+payloads) and idle time does not recover the memory.
+
+Allocate the pool payloads with alloc_skb() instead: each payload gets
+its own kmalloc object (kmalloc-4096 for the default 1792-byte medium
+profile buffer), freed in full the moment the skb is freed, bounding
+pool memory at one page per buffer with zero cross-lifetime pinning.
+The skb geometry is unchanged (same payload_len mapping, NET_SKB_PAD
+headroom restored via skb_reserve; the firmware reports payload_offs
+relative to head on return, and tailroom only grows). Allocation cost
+moves from page-frag carve to slab alloc on the refill path only,
+which runs at pool-churn rate, not per-packet rate.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+--- a/nss_core.c
++++ b/nss_core.c
+@@ -2023,7 +2023,18 @@
+ 		dma_addr_t buffer;
+ 		struct h2n_descriptor *desc = &desc_ring[hlos_index];
+ 
+-		struct sk_buff *nbuf = dev_alloc_skb(max_buf_size);
++		/*
++		 * These skbs are handed to the NSS firmware, which keeps
++		 * them in its empty-buffer pool indefinitely. They must not
++		 * come from the shared per-CPU page-frag caches
++		 * (dev_alloc_skb): a long-held ~2KB payload pins its whole
++		 * 32KB frag chunk once the neighbouring traffic buffers are
++		 * freed, which measured as ~5x RAM amplification on a 512MB
++		 * IPQ8074 (~200MB pinned for ~35MB of payloads). alloc_skb()
++		 * backs each payload with its own kmalloc object instead,
++		 * which is returned in full the moment the skb is freed.
++		 */
++		struct sk_buff *nbuf = alloc_skb(payload_len, GFP_ATOMIC);
+ 		if (unlikely(!nbuf)) {
+ 			/*
+ 			 * ERR:
+@@ -2032,6 +2043,7 @@
+ 			nss_warning("%px: Could not obtain empty buffer", nss_ctx);
+ 			break;
+ 		}
++		skb_reserve(nbuf, NET_SKB_PAD);
+ 
+ 		/*
+ 		 * Map the skb
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0106-nss_init-tear-down-module-global-registrations-on-exit.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0106-nss_init-tear-down-module-global-registrations-on-exit.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f44517c9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0106-nss_init-tear-down-module-global-registrations-on-exit.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_init: tear down module-global registrations on exit
+
+nss_cleanup() leaves three pieces of global kernel state behind, all
+pointing into module memory that is freed by the rmmod:
+
+- the dev/nss/stats sysctl header: the only sysctl table in the driver
+  without an unregister counterpart. The next insmod registers
+  dev/nss/stats again, and insert_header() strlen()s a procname read
+  from the leaked entry's recycled memory:
+
+    __pi_strlen <- __register_sysctl_table <- register_sysctl_sz
+    <- nss_stats_register_sysctl <- init_module [qca_nss_drv]
+
+- the panic notifier block: nss_coredump_notify_register() has no
+  unregister anywhere. The next insmod walks the chain over the stale
+  node when inserting its own:
+
+    notifier_chain_register <- nss_coredump_notify_register
+    <- init_module [qca_nss_drv]
+
+  and any panic in between would call into freed module text.
+
+- the "coredump_wait" singlethread workqueue, which also leaks one
+  kernel thread per reload cycle.
+
+- the qca-nss-drv debugfs tree: it is created at init, but
+  nss_stats_clean() only runs from the per-device remove path. With the
+  core probes deferred (or after any unbind), module exit leaves the
+  root behind, every later load logs "debugfs: 'qca-nss-drv' already
+  exists" and comes up with a broken stats tree.
+
+The driver normally loads once per boot, so none of this shows until
+the module is reloaded - both crash signatures above reproduce on a
+plain rmmod/modprobe cycle. Add the missing unregister paths and call
+them from nss_cleanup(); the coredump teardown runs after
+platform_driver_unregister() so no device interrupt can queue the wait
+work while it is being destroyed.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+
+---
+diff -ruN a/nss_core.h b/nss_core.h
+--- a/nss_core.h
++++ b/nss_core.h
+@@ -1055,6 +1055,7 @@
+  * APIs for coredump
+  */
+ extern void nss_coredump_notify_register(void);
++extern void nss_coredump_notify_unregister(void);
+ extern void nss_fw_coredump_notify(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_own, int intr);
+ extern int nss_coredump_init_delay_work(void);
+ 
+diff -ruN a/nss_coredump.c b/nss_coredump.c
+--- a/nss_coredump.c
++++ b/nss_coredump.c
+@@ -134,6 +134,24 @@
+ }
+ 
+ /*
++ * nss_coredump_notify_unregister()
++ *	Tear down what nss_coredump_notify_register() and
++ *	nss_coredump_init_delay_work() registered globally. Called on module
++ *	exit after the platform driver is unregistered, so no interrupt can
++ *	queue the wait work anymore.
++ */
++void nss_coredump_notify_unregister(void)
++{
++	if (coredump_workqueue) {
++		cancel_delayed_work_sync(&coredump_queuewait);
++		destroy_workqueue(coredump_workqueue);
++		coredump_workqueue = NULL;
++	}
++
++	atomic_notifier_chain_unregister(&panic_notifier_list, &nss_panic_nb);
++}
++
++/*
+  * nss_fw_coredump_notify()
+  *	handler for coredump notification from NSS FW
+  */
+diff -ruN a/nss_init.c b/nss_init.c
+--- a/nss_init.c
++++ b/nss_init.c
+@@ -1029,6 +1029,11 @@
+ 	nss_ipv6_free_conn_tables();
+ #endif
+ 
++	/*
++	 * Unregister the non-zero-stats sysctl
++	 */
++	nss_stats_unregister_sysctl();
++
+ 	nss_project_unregister_sysctl();
+ 
+ #ifdef NSS_DATA_PLANE_GENERIC_SUPPORT
+@@ -1047,6 +1052,19 @@
+ #endif
+ 
+ 	platform_driver_unregister(&nss_driver);
++
++	/*
++	 * The debugfs tree is created at init but nss_stats_clean() only runs
++	 * from the per-device remove path - with the probe deferred it never
++	 * ran, and the leaked root breaks debugfs for every later load.
++	 */
++	nss_stats_clean();
++
++	/*
++	 * All device interrupts are gone now - drop the panic notifier and
++	 * the coredump wait workqueue registered at init.
++	 */
++	nss_coredump_notify_unregister();
+ }
+ 
+ module_init(nss_init);
+diff -ruN a/nss_stats.c b/nss_stats.c
+--- a/nss_stats.c
++++ b/nss_stats.c
+@@ -135,6 +135,18 @@
+ }
+ 
+ /*
++ * nss_stats_unregister_sysctl()
++ *	Unregister the sysctl table for stats.
++ */
++void nss_stats_unregister_sysctl(void)
++{
++	if (nss_stats_header) {
++		unregister_sysctl_table(nss_stats_header);
++		nss_stats_header = NULL;
++	}
++}
++
++/*
+  * nss_stats_open()
+  *	Opens stats file.
+  */
+diff -ruN a/nss_stats.h b/nss_stats.h
+--- a/nss_stats.h
++++ b/nss_stats.h
+@@ -69,6 +69,7 @@
+ };
+ 
+ extern void nss_stats_register_sysctl(void);
++extern void nss_stats_unregister_sysctl(void);
+ void nss_stats_init(void);
+ extern int nss_stats_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp);
+ extern int nss_stats_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp);
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0107-nss_hal-hold-the-core-in-reset-on-device-remove.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0107-nss_hal-hold-the-core-in-reset-on-device-remove.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..5685a3ce
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0107-nss_hal-hold-the-core-in-reset-on-device-remove.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_hal: hold the core in reset on device remove
+
+nss_hal_remove() tears down the host side - interrupts, data plane,
+mappings - but never stops the NSS core, so the firmware keeps executing
+out of its DDR region with no host attached. On the next probe,
+firmware_load then copies the new image over the memory the old
+firmware is still running from. The firmware traps (UBI32 trap dump in
+the kernel log, PCs in IMEM), raises COREDUMP_COMPLETE into the
+interrupt line the probe has just requested, and the coredump handler
+takes the whole system down:
+
+  NSS core 0 signal COREDUMP COMPLETE 4000
+  Kernel panic - not syncing: NSS FW coredump: bringing system down
+
+50ms after the "fw of size ... copied to addr: 40000000" line, and
+before core_reset() would have put the core into reset for the actual
+restart. This makes any driver reload with armed cores a guaranteed
+panic and firmware re-arm unusable as a runtime recovery.
+
+Add a core_halt HAL op that runs the exact reverse of core_reset() -
+assert the ubi32 reset clamp, then both GCC reset sets - and halt ALL
+booted cores first thing in the FIRST nss_hal_remove() call: the driver
+core unbinds in reverse bind order, so core 1's remove runs first, and
+its data_plane_unregister() tears down every core's physical interfaces
+in one pass - the shared-EDMA baseline restore that follows the last
+release must not race a still-running core 0. The clamp alone is not enough: it
+stops instruction fetch but leaves the core complex powered through
+its GCC resets, and a firmware booted over that half-reset state
+either traps right after its boot handshake or hangs in early init.
+Fully back in reset, the core sits across the module reload in the
+same state a cold boot starts from, the qca-ppe-nss glue hands the
+shared EDMA block back to the host during the data plane unregister
+that follows, and the next probe boots the new image cleanly.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+
+---
+--- a/nss_hal/include/nss_hal_ops.h
++++ b/nss_hal/include/nss_hal_ops.h
+@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
+ struct nss_hal_ops {
+ 	int (*common_reset)(struct platform_device *pdev);
+ 	int (*core_reset)(struct platform_device *nss_dev, void __iomem *map, uint32_t addr, uint32_t clk_src);
++	void (*core_halt)(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx);
+ 	int (*clock_configure)(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx, struct platform_device *nss_dev, struct nss_platform_data *npd);
+ 	void (*debug_enable)(void);
+ 	struct nss_platform_data * (*of_get_pdata)(struct platform_device *pdev);
+--- a/nss_hal/ipq807x/nss_hal_pvt.c
++++ b/nss_hal/ipq807x/nss_hal_pvt.c
+@@ -367,6 +367,37 @@
+ }
+ 
+ /*
++ * __nss_hal_core_halt()
++ *	Put the core back into the reset state a cold boot starts from.
++ *
++ * Exact reverse of __nss_hal_core_reset(): assert the ubi32 reset clamp,
++ * then both GCC reset sets. The clamp alone is not enough - it stops
++ * instruction fetch but leaves the core complex powered through its GCC
++ * resets, and a firmware image booted over that half-reset state either
++ * traps right after its boot handshake or hangs waiting in early init.
++ * With the GCC resets asserted the next probe's core_reset() runs the
++ * same de-assert sequence as on a cold boot.
++ */
++static void __nss_hal_core_halt(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx)
++{
++	uint32_t value;
++
++	nss_write_32(nss_ctx->nmap, NSS_REGS_RESET_CTRL_OFFSET, 1);
++
++	if (!nss_misc_reset)
++		return;
++
++	value = nss_read_32(nss_misc_reset, 0x0);
++	value |= (NSS_CORE_GCC_RESET_2 << (nss_ctx->id << 3));
++	nss_write_32(nss_misc_reset, 0x0, value);
++
++	usleep_range(10, 20);
++
++	value |= (NSS_CORE_GCC_RESET_1 << (nss_ctx->id << 3));
++	nss_write_32(nss_misc_reset, 0x0, value);
++}
++
++/*
+  * __nss_hal_debug_enable()
+  *	Enable NSS debug
+  */
+@@ -794,6 +825,7 @@
+ struct nss_hal_ops nss_hal_ipq807x_ops = {
+ 	.common_reset = __nss_hal_common_reset,
+ 	.core_reset = __nss_hal_core_reset,
++	.core_halt = __nss_hal_core_halt,
+ 	.clock_configure = __nss_hal_clock_configure,
+ 	.firmware_load = nss_hal_firmware_load,
+ 	.debug_enable = __nss_hal_debug_enable,
+--- a/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
++++ b/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
+@@ -895,6 +895,43 @@
+ 	int i;
+ 
+ 	/*
++	 * Stop the firmware first: without this the cores keep executing
++	 * after the rmmod, and the next probe's firmware_load copies the
++	 * new image over the running firmware's memory - it traps and
++	 * signals a coredump into the freshly requested interrupt line,
++	 * panicking the system before core_reset() would have restarted
++	 * the core.
++	 *
++	 * ALL cores must go into reset here, on the FIRST device remove:
++	 * the driver core unbinds in reverse bind order (core 1 first),
++	 * but data_plane_unregister() below tears down every core's
++	 * physical interfaces in one pass, and the qca-ppe-nss glue hands
++	 * the shared EDMA block back to the host when the last one is
++	 * released - core 0 must already be stopped at that point. Halt
++	 * each core exactly once: the register block of a core whose GCC
++	 * resets are already asserted must not be touched again.
++	 *
++	 * The latch is scoped to the module lifetime rather than to one
++	 * unbind sequence, so an unbind, rebind and second unbind through
++	 * sysfs would skip the halt. That is the intended scope, not an
++	 * oversight: this exists to make the detach clean, not to make
++	 * rebinding work - re-arming the firmware without a module reload
++	 * is a separate unsolved problem - and a module reload clears the
++	 * latch along with the rest of the module's state.
++	 */
++	if (nss_top->hal_ops->core_halt) {
++		static bool cores_halted;
++
++		if (!cores_halted) {
++			for (i = 0; i < nss_top->num_nss; i++) {
++				if (nss_top->nss[i].nmap)
++					nss_top->hal_ops->core_halt(&nss_top->nss[i]);
++			}
++			cores_halted = true;
++		}
++	}
++
++	/*
+ 	 * Clean up debugfs
+ 	 */
+ 	nss_stats_clean();
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0108-nss_coredump-dump-the-whole-firmware-log-ring.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0108-nss_coredump-dump-the-whole-firmware-log-ring.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..543a12b0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0108-nss_coredump-dump-the-whole-firmware-log-ring.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,284 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_coredump: dump the whole firmware log ring
+
+When the NSS firmware coredumps, nss_fw_coredump_notify() prints the
+last NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM entries of the firmware's log ring, a
+hardcoded 25. The ring the firmware publishes through meminfo holds 32
+entries on ipq807x (debug_boot_log_desc, 0x1420 bytes over a 160-byte
+entry), so a single firmware trap - which logs five lines per trap and
+retries - fills the window on its own and the dump contains nothing but
+the trap. Whatever the firmware was doing beforehand, which is the only
+thing that identifies the fault, has already been printed over.
+
+Dump the ring instead of a fixed slice, and print the sequence number,
+thread and timestamp the firmware already records in every entry so a
+burst can be told apart from activity spread over hours, and so a
+wrapped ring is visible as such. The live readout in nss_log_read()
+already prints index and timestamp; only the coredump path dropped them.
+
+The descriptor is written by DMA from a firmware that has just died, so
+take the entry count from the ring the driver itself allocated where
+there is one, and refuse a zero count rather than deriving indices from
+it: the previous code would divide by zero on a zero count, and a count
+above INT_MAX made curr_index negative and walked the print outside the
+buffer.
+
+For the meminfo ring there is no driver-allocated count, but there is a
+driver-known size: nss_meminfo_init_block_lists() has the region's size
+in hand when it stores the pointer, and threw it away. Keep it, and bound
+log_nentries by it using the same stride the print walk indexes with.
+Without that the count is a firmware field bounded only by a constant
+picked independently of the allocation - 1024 against a 32-entry ring -
+so a corrupt descriptor makes the panic path read, and printk, some
+159 KB past a 5152-byte mapping, on the one code path whose whole purpose
+is to survive a firmware fault intact.
+
+The ring the driver allocates carries the same hazard from the other
+direction: nentry comes from the dev.nss.general.logbuf sysctl, which
+only refuses values below 32, and sizeof(descriptor) + sizeof(entry) *
+nentry wrapped a uint32_t well before kmalloc could refuse it -
+publishing a ring count far larger than the allocation the coredump walk
+then indexes. struct_size_t() saturates instead, so an implausible
+request fails the allocation and no count is published.
+
+Print the newest NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_MAX entries of a larger ring
+rather than calling the ring unusable and skipping the dump: a fw_logbuf
+above 1024 silently turned off the forensics this patch exists for.
+Both the window and the print run from the newest entry backwards, so a
+budget cut drops the oldest lines, never the ones immediately before the
+trap.
+
+Bound the print in time as well. The notifier arms one
+nss_coredump_wait() covering every core and then prints from the NAPI
+poll, so a print that outruns its share of that window holds off the
+other core's COREDUMP COMPLETE until the backstop panic has already
+truncated the dump. Measured on a 115200 console, 256 entries cost 2.58s
+against a 3456ms wait, and the second core's completion arrived 2.59s
+late as a direct result. Stop at a fraction of the wait and say how many
+entries were printed; because the walk is newest-first, the rest are the
+oldest.
+
+The ring allocation moves to GFP_KERNEL - its only caller is the
+dev.nss.general.logbuf sysctl write handler, which runs in process
+context, and an atomic allocation is what would fail for a larger ring.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+---
+--- a/nss_coredump.c
++++ b/nss_coredump.c
+@@ -32,12 +32,24 @@
+ #include <linux/panic_notifier.h>	/* for panic_notifier_list */
+ #endif
+ #include <linux/jiffies.h>	/* for time */
++#include <linux/sched/clock.h>	/* for local_clock */
+ #include "nss_tx_rx_common.h"
+ 
+ #if NSS_MAX_CORES > 2	/* see comment in nss_fw_coredump_notify */
+ #error	too many NSS Cores: should be 1 or 2
+ #endif
+ 
++/*
++ * How long nss_fw_coredump_notify() gives the remaining cores to signal that
++ * they have finished dumping, and how much of that each core's log print may
++ * spend. The notifier prints from the NAPI poll, so a print that outruns its
++ * share holds off the other core's COREDUMP COMPLETE until the backstop panic
++ * has already truncated the dump this code exists to produce: 256 entries on a
++ * 115200 console measured 2.58s, against the 3456ms below.
++ */
++#define NSS_COREDUMP_WAIT_MS		3456
++#define NSS_COREDUMP_LOG_BUDGET_MS	(NSS_COREDUMP_WAIT_MS / (NSS_MAX_CORES * 2))
++
+ static struct delayed_work coredump_queuewait;
+ static struct workqueue_struct *coredump_workqueue;
+ 
+@@ -159,6 +171,8 @@ void nss_fw_coredump_notify(struct nss_c
+ 				int intr __attribute__ ((unused)))
+ {
+ 	int i, j, curr_index, useful_entries, num_cores_wait;
++	uint32_t nentries, current_entry;
++	u64 budget;
+ 	struct nss_log_descriptor *nld;
+ 	struct nss_log_entry *nle_init, *nle_print;
+ 	dma_addr_t dma_addr;
+@@ -168,51 +182,80 @@ void nss_fw_coredump_notify(struct nss_c
+ 			nss_own, intr, nss_own->nmap, nss_own->state);
+ 	nss_own->state |= NSS_CORE_STATE_FW_DEAD;
+ 	queue_delayed_work(coredump_workqueue, &coredump_queuewait,
+-			msecs_to_jiffies(3456));
++			msecs_to_jiffies(NSS_COREDUMP_WAIT_MS));
+ 
+ 	/*
+ 	 * If external log buffer is not set, use the nss initial log buffer.
+ 	 */
+ 	nld = (struct nss_log_descriptor *)(nss_rbe[nss_own->id].addr);
+ 	dma_addr = nss_rbe[nss_own->id].dma_addr;
++	nentries = nss_rbe[nss_own->id].nentries;
+ 	if (!nld) {
+ 		nld = nss_own->meminfo_ctx.logbuffer;
+ 		dma_addr = nss_own->meminfo_ctx.logbuffer_dma;
++
++		/*
++		 * log_nentries sits in the block the firmware writes, and the
++		 * firmware has just trapped. The block is ours, so bound the
++		 * count by what we actually mapped, using the stride the walk
++		 * below indexes with.
++		 */
++		nentries = nld ? min_t(uint32_t, nld->log_nentries,
++			(nss_own->meminfo_ctx.logbuffer_size -
++				offsetof(struct nss_log_descriptor, log_ring_buffer)) /
++					sizeof(struct nss_log_entry)) : 0;
++	}
++
++	if (!nld) {
++		nss_info_always("%px: no NSS-FW logbuffer for core %u\n",
++				nss_own, nss_own->id);
++		goto dumped;
+ 	}
+ 
+ 	dma_sync_single_for_cpu(nss_own->dev, dma_addr, sizeof(struct nss_log_descriptor), DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+ 
++	if (!nentries) {
++		nss_info_always("%px: NSS-FW logbuffer for core %u holds no entries\n",
++				nss_own, nss_own->id);
++		goto dumped;
++	}
++
+ 	/*
+-	 * If the current entry is smaller than or equal to the number of NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM,
+-	 * only print whatever is in the buffer. Otherwise, dump last NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM
+-	 * to the dmessage.
++	 * Walk the newest entries backwards from current_entry, so a ring
++	 * larger than the print cap loses its oldest lines rather than all
++	 * of them.
+ 	 */
+-	nss_info_always("%px: Starting NSS-FW logbuffer dump for core %u\n",
+-			nss_own, nss_own->id);
+-	nle_init = nld->log_ring_buffer;
+-	if (nld->current_entry <= NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM) {
+-		curr_index = 0;
+-		useful_entries = nld->current_entry;
+-	} else {
+-		curr_index = ((nld->current_entry - NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM) % nld->log_nentries);
+-		useful_entries = NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM;
+-	}
++	current_entry = nld->current_entry;
++	useful_entries = min3(current_entry, nentries,
++			      (uint32_t)NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_MAX);
++	curr_index = (current_entry - 1) % nentries;
+ 
++	nss_info_always("%px: Starting NSS-FW logbuffer dump for core %u (ring %u entries, at %u, newest first)\n",
++			nss_own, nss_own->id, nentries, current_entry);
++	nle_init = nld->log_ring_buffer;
+ 	nle_print = nle_init + curr_index;
+-	for (j = index = curr_index; j < (curr_index + useful_entries); j++, index++) {
+-		if (j == nld->log_nentries) {
+-			nle_print = nle_init;
+-			index = 0;
++
++	budget = local_clock() + NSS_COREDUMP_LOG_BUDGET_MS * NSEC_PER_MSEC;
++	for (j = 0, index = curr_index; j < useful_entries; j++) {
++		if (local_clock() > budget) {
++			nss_info_always("%px: NSS-FW logbuffer dump for core %u cut short after %d of %d entries, oldest dropped\n",
++					nss_own, nss_own->id, j, useful_entries);
++			break;
+ 		}
+ 
+ 		offset = (index * sizeof(struct nss_log_entry))
+ 			+ offsetof(struct nss_log_descriptor, log_ring_buffer);
+ 		dma_sync_single_for_cpu(nss_own->dev, dma_addr + offset,
+ 				sizeof(struct nss_log_entry), DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
+-		nss_info_always("%px: %s\n", nss_own, nle_print->message);
+-		nle_print++;
++		nss_info_always("%px: %llu %3u %010u: %s\n", nss_own,
++				nle_print->sequence_num, nle_print->thread_num,
++				nle_print->timestamp, nle_print->message);
++
++		index = index ? index - 1 : nentries - 1;
++		nle_print = nle_init + index;
+ 	}
+ 
++dumped:
+ 	if (nss_own->state & NSS_CORE_STATE_PANIC)
+ 		return;
+ 
+--- a/nss_log.c
++++ b/nss_log.c
+@@ -399,7 +399,7 @@ bool nss_debug_log_buffer_alloc(uint8_t
+ 	struct nss_top_instance *nss_top;
+ 	struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx;
+ 	dma_addr_t dma_addr;
+-	uint32_t size;
++	size_t size;
+ 	void *addr = NULL;
+ 	nss_tx_status_t status;
+ 
+@@ -415,10 +415,16 @@ bool nss_debug_log_buffer_alloc(uint8_t
+ 		return false;
+ 	}
+ 
+-	size = sizeof(struct nss_log_descriptor) + (sizeof(struct nss_log_entry) * nentry);
+-	addr = kmalloc(size, GFP_ATOMIC);
++	/*
++	 * nentry comes from the dev.nss.general.logbuf sysctl, which has no
++	 * upper bound: saturate rather than wrap, so an implausible request
++	 * fails the allocation instead of publishing a ring count that the
++	 * coredump walk would then index past the end of.
++	 */
++	size = struct_size_t(struct nss_log_descriptor, log_ring_buffer, nentry);
++	addr = kmalloc(size, GFP_KERNEL);
+ 	if (!addr) {
+-		nss_warning("%px: Failed to allocate memory for logging (size:%d)\n", nss_ctx, size);
++		nss_warning("%px: Failed to allocate memory for logging (size:%zu)\n", nss_ctx, size);
+ 		return false;
+ 	}
+ 
+--- a/nss_log.h
++++ b/nss_log.h
+@@ -33,6 +33,13 @@ enum nss_debug_interface_msg_type {
+ };
+ 
+ /*
++ * Upper bound on the number of firmware log entries printed during a
++ * coredump. The ring size decides how much is actually dumped; a larger
++ * ring is printed from its newest entry backwards.
++ */
++#define NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_MAX	1024
++
++/*
+  * The size of each log entry to be displayed.
+  */
+ #define	NSS_LOG_OUTPUT_LINE_SIZE	151	/* 5 + 12 + 132 + '\n' + '\0' (see below) */
+@@ -41,13 +48,6 @@ enum nss_debug_interface_msg_type {
+ #define	NSS_LOG_COOKIE			0xFF785634
+ 
+ /*
+- * Dump last N entry during the coredump.
+- * This number should be lower than the minimum size of the logbuf
+- * which 32 right now.
+- */
+-#define NSS_LOG_COREDUMP_LINE_NUM	25
+-
+-/*
+  * Saves the ring buffer address for logging per NSS core
+  */
+ struct nss_log_ring_buffer_addr {
+--- a/nss_meminfo.c
++++ b/nss_meminfo.c
+@@ -398,6 +398,7 @@ static bool nss_meminfo_init_block_lists
+ 		if (!strcmp(r->name, "debug_boot_log_desc")) {
+ 			BUG_ON(mtype == NSS_MEMINFO_MEMTYPE_UTCM_SHARED);
+ 			mem_ctx->logbuffer_memtype = mtype;
++			mem_ctx->logbuffer_size = r->size;
+ 			mem_ctx->logbuffer_dma = dma_addr;
+ 			mem_ctx->logbuffer = (struct nss_log_descriptor *)kern_addr;
+ 		}
+--- a/nss_meminfo.h
++++ b/nss_meminfo.h
+@@ -120,6 +120,7 @@ struct nss_meminfo_ctx {
+ 	uint32_t if_map_dma;				/* nss_if_mem_map_inst physical address */
+ 	enum nss_meminfo_memtype if_map_memtype;	/* Memory type for nss_if_mem_map */
+ 	struct nss_log_descriptor *logbuffer;		/* nss_logbuffer virtual address */
++	uint32_t logbuffer_size;			/* nss_logbuffer block size */
+ 	uint32_t logbuffer_dma;				/* nss_logbuffer physical address */
+ 	enum nss_meminfo_memtype logbuffer_memtype;	/* Memory type for logbuffer */
+ 	uint32_t c2c_start_dma;				/* nss_c2c start physical address */
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0109-nss_phys_if-serialise-sync-message-responses.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0109-nss_phys_if-serialise-sync-message-responses.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..38ae8b7f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0109-nss_phys_if-serialise-sync-message-responses.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_phys_if: do not answer a request with the previous one's response
+
+Every physical-interface control message - open, close, mac_addr,
+change_mtu, link_state, and the interface-base messages that go through
+the same path - is sent by nss_phys_if_msg_sync(), which serialises
+callers on one semaphore and then waits on a single file-static
+completion for the reply.
+
+The completion is initialised once, at first use, and never reset. On
+the 3 second timeout the sender abandons its request and reports
+NSS_TX_FAILURE, but the request is still queued to the firmware: when
+the reply eventually arrives, nss_phys_if_callback() writes the shared
+response and calls complete() with nobody waiting, leaving the
+completion signalled. From that point the path is permanently skewed by
+one - the next sender's wait_for_completion_timeout() returns
+immediately, consuming that leftover token without waiting for its own
+reply, and reads the previous request's result out of the equally shared
+response field. Its own reply then re-signals the completion, so the
+skew sustains itself for as long as the module is loaded. Nothing short
+of a reboot restores it, and none of it is visible: the driver builds
+with debug messages compiled out.
+
+The effect on the caller is that a control message reports success it
+never had. A port whose link-up notification is reported as delivered,
+but which the firmware never applied, is left with the host convinced
+the port can transmit while the firmware silently discards everything
+sent to it.
+
+Give each request a serial, stamp it into the message's app_data so the
+firmware echoes it back, and drop responses in the callback that are no
+longer the one being waited for. Clear the completion before sending as
+well, so a request never starts against a leftover token.
+
+Count both events and expose them as module parameters. The messages
+beside them compile out at the default debug level, which is the reason
+this went unseen; a box that has never timed out reads zero on both, so
+the counters also say when this is not what went wrong.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+
+--- a/nss_phys_if.c
++++ b/nss_phys_if.c
+@@ -41,11 +41,27 @@
+ 	struct semaphore sem;
+ 	struct completion complete;
+ 	int response;
++	nss_ptr_t serial;
+ } phif;
+ 
+ static int nss_phys_if_sem_init_done;
+ 
+ /*
++ * Both counters stay zero on a healthy box. A nonzero timeout count is the
++ * event that used to corrupt every later request; a nonzero stale count is this
++ * driver discarding a response that arrived too late to be the awaited one.
++ * They are module parameters because the debug messages beside them compile out
++ * at the default debug level, which is why this failure was never visible.
++ */
++static unsigned int phif_timeouts;
++module_param(phif_timeouts, uint, S_IRUGO);
++MODULE_PARM_DESC(phif_timeouts, "phys_if synchronous messages that timed out");
++
++static unsigned int phif_stale_responses;
++module_param(phif_stale_responses, uint, S_IRUGO);
++MODULE_PARM_DESC(phif_stale_responses, "phys_if responses discarded as stale");
++
++/*
+  * nss_phys_if_update_driver_stats()
+  *	Snoop the extended message and update driver statistics.
+  */
+@@ -145,6 +161,23 @@
+  */
+ static void nss_phys_if_callback(void *app_data, struct nss_phys_if_msg *nim)
+ {
++	/*
++	 * A request that timed out is abandoned by its sender but stays in
++	 * flight, so its response can arrive after the next request has already
++	 * been sent. Answering that one with this response would report the
++	 * wrong result for it and, worse, leave the completion signalled with
++	 * no waiter, after which every later request returns immediately with
++	 * its predecessor's status - permanently, because nothing resets it.
++	 * The serial is stamped into app_data by the sender, so a response that
++	 * is no longer the one being waited for is simply dropped here.
++	 */
++	if ((nss_ptr_t)app_data != phif.serial) {
++		phif_stale_responses++;
++		nss_warning("phys_if stale response for serial %px, current %px\n",
++				app_data, (void *)phif.serial);
++		return;
++	}
++
+ 	if(nim->cm.response != NSS_CMN_RESPONSE_ACK) {
+ 		nss_warning("phys_if Error response %d\n", nim->cm.response);
+ 		phif.response = NSS_TX_FAILURE;
+@@ -225,6 +258,15 @@
+ 
+ 	down(&phif.sem);
+ 
++	/*
++	 * Identify this request and start from a cleared completion, so that a
++	 * late response belonging to an earlier, timed-out request cannot be
++	 * mistaken for this one's.
++	 */
++	phif.serial++;
++	nim->cm.app_data = phif.serial;
++	reinit_completion(&phif.complete);
++
+ 	status = nss_phys_if_msg(nss_ctx, nim);
+ 	if(status != NSS_TX_SUCCESS)
+ 	{
+@@ -237,6 +279,7 @@
+ 
+ 	if(!ret)
+ 	{
++		phif_timeouts++;
+ 		nss_warning("%px: phys_if tx failed due to timeout\n", nss_ctx);
+ 		phif.response = NSS_TX_FAILURE;
+ 	}
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0110-nss_data_plane-do-not-skip-open-after-a-failed-close.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0110-nss_data_plane-do-not-skip-open-after-a-failed-close.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c7b0e4df
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0110-nss_data_plane-do-not-skip-open-after-a-failed-close.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+Subject: [PATCH] nss_data_plane: do not skip open after a close that failed
+
+The data plane keeps a per-interface notify_open shadow so that a
+repeated open or close costs no firmware round trip. Open is skipped
+when the shadow says the firmware interface is already open, and close
+is skipped when it says it is already closed.
+
+The shadow is only updated when the message succeeds. A close that fails
+therefore leaves it reading "open" - and the very next open returns
+NSS_DP_SUCCESS without sending anything to the firmware at all.
+
+That is the wrong way round for this message pair. nss_phys_if_close()
+is synchronous: it sends NSS_PHYS_IF_CLOSE and then waits three seconds
+for the reply. When it reports failure the usual reason is that the
+reply did not arrive in time - the message itself was already handed to
+the firmware, and the firmware very probably did close the interface.
+So the one case where the shadow is most likely to be wrong is exactly
+the case where it is left untouched, and it is left claiming the state
+that suppresses the message which would repair it.
+
+The consequence is a physical port that never transmits again. Its
+firmware interface is shut while the host believes it is open, so the
+host and the firmware both keep accepting frames for it and none reach
+the wire: carrier up, the MAC's receive counters climbing and its
+transmit counters at exactly zero, the firmware's per-port egress
+counter frozen, and no error reported by anything. A link cycle does not
+touch it, because a link-state notification is not an open. Tearing the
+data plane down and bringing it back does not touch it either, because
+the open on the way back is the one being skipped. Only a reboot clears
+it, since nothing else re-boots the firmware or reloads the driver.
+
+Clear the shadow whichever way the close goes. A close that failed
+leaves the firmware's state unknown, and the safe reading of unknown is
+"assume it is closed": an open sent to an interface that is already open
+costs one message, while an open withheld from one that is closed costs
+the port. This is the same discipline the glue already applies to link
+state, where a notification the firmware did not acknowledge obliges the
+next one to be sent rather than skipped.
+
+Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
+---
+--- a/nss_data_plane/nss_data_plane.c
++++ b/nss_data_plane/nss_data_plane.c
+@@ -65,8 +65,21 @@
+ 		return NSS_DP_SUCCESS;
+ 	}
+ 
++	/*
++	 * Drop the shadow before the message rather than after it succeeds.
++	 * nss_phys_if_close() waits for the firmware's reply and reports
++	 * failure when it times out, by which point the close itself has
++	 * already been sent - so a failure here means the interface's state is
++	 * unknown, not that it is still open. Leaving the shadow set would let
++	 * the next open short-circuit and send nothing, and a firmware
++	 * interface left shut under a host that believes it is open never
++	 * transmits again until the box is rebooted. An open sent to an
++	 * already-open interface costs one message; an open withheld from a
++	 * closed one costs the port.
++	 */
++	dp->notify_open = 0;
++
+ 	if (nss_phys_if_close(dp->nss_ctx, dp->if_num) == NSS_TX_SUCCESS) {
+-		dp->notify_open = 0;
+ 		return NSS_DP_SUCCESS;
+ 	}
+ 	return NSS_DP_FAILURE;
diff --git a/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0111-nss_core-bound-the-N2H-descriptor-geometry.patch b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0111-nss_core-bound-the-N2H-descriptor-geometry.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..e56bf14f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/qca-nss-drv/patches/0111-nss_core-bound-the-N2H-descriptor-geometry.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,170 @@
+nss_core: bound the N2H descriptor geometry before shaping the skb
+
+payload_offs and payload_len arrive in the N2H descriptor, which the NSS
+firmware writes into host memory. Both are uint16_t, and the linear
+receive path uses them without any bound:
+
+	nbuf->data = nbuf->head + desc->payload_offs;
+	nbuf->len = desc->payload_len;
+	skb_set_tail_pointer(nbuf, nbuf->len);
+
+The buffer they describe is the alloc_skb(max_buf_size + NET_SKB_PAD)
+the host handed to the firmware, mapped in full from nbuf->head - a
+little over 2 KB. Nothing re-derives that size here, so a descriptor
+naming an offset and a length inside 64 KB shapes an skb that claims up
+to 64 KB starting past the allocation. That skb then goes to
+eth_type_trans() and netif_receive_skb(), or to the wifili data-plane
+callback: adjacent slab contents can be forwarded onto the LAN or up the
+PPPoE link. The dma_unmap_single() a few lines further down invalidates
+the same out-of-range span, and the NSS node carries no dma-coherent, so
+that is real cache maintenance over unrelated kernel objects.
+
+The paged sibling has the bound written down already:
+
+	nss_assert(desc->payload_offs + desc->payload_len <= PAGE_SIZE);
+
+but the package builds with NSS_DEBUG_LEVEL=0, where nss_assert() expands
+to nothing, and __skb_fill_page_desc() runs on the unvalidated pair
+regardless.
+
+The same asymmetry costs a minimum as well as a maximum. The paged path
+ends at a pull label that refuses a frame shorter than an ethernet
+header, because the data-plane callbacks all pull one without checking;
+the linear path has no equivalent, so a payload_len below ETH_HLEN
+reaches eth_type_trans(), whose skb_pull_inline() underflows skb->len to
+roughly 4 GiB before napi_gro_receive() sees it. Check that once at the
+dispatch point, where both paths converge and the buffer type is known,
+rather than a second time in each handler.
+
+Check the geometry against the size each path actually allocated and
+mapped, and drop the descriptor the way the existing error paths in these
+two handlers do - unmap what was mapped, decrement the in-firmware skb
+count, free, and return false so the caller skips the consume.
+
+The same buffer feeds nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt(), which reads
+ncm->interface, ncm->version and ncm->len before anything establishes the
+buffer is even as large as the header - including the ncm->len check
+itself. Take the length from whichever branch supplied the pointer and
+test it first.
+
+And nss_core_handle_nr_frag_skb() appends every non-first segment to the
+head skb with skb_add_rx_frag(jumbo_start, skb_shinfo(jumbo_start)->nr_frags,
+...) while the firmware alone decides how many segments it sends, so a
+chain longer than MAX_SKB_FRAGS walks off the head's frag array. Bound it
+and drop the chain the way the sibling "full frame before a last" path
+already does.
